# House-style wording for Crisis 02 (#83). SPOILERS.
TEXT = {
 "briefing": {"dateLine": "The 5th. Winter, three months after IRON HARVEST.", "situation": [
   "Your cell now works for Under Secretary H. Marlowe, who handles the alliance's relationship with Elsin.",
   "At 0710 this morning a man walked into our embassy in Aubrec, the capital of neutral Pellaria, and asked for asylum. He says he is a major on the planning directorate of Kordav's General Staff. The station has given him the cryptonym LANTERN.",
   "In Kalsa, Elsin's parliament is about to vote on a no-confidence motion against Deputy Defence Minister Aron Vell. Vell negotiated the task force agreement, and Kordav would be glad to see him go.",
   "From here on, only public reporting and whatever LANTERN chooses to volunteer reach you unasked. Everything secret has to be tasked: questioning him, signals intelligence, your own sources. Assume Kordav knows some of what you can collect."]},
 "customer": {"context": "Careful and political, and she dislikes surprises. Anything she passes to Kalsa is hard to take back. The full allegation can't be taken back at all."},
 "replies": {
   "share_full": "I have passed it to Kalsa in full, the ledger included. It can't be recalled. If it is wrong, it is the most damaging thing I have signed.",
   "share_caveat": "I have told Elsin's minister that we hold single-source, unverified reporting about Vell. He asked what he should do with that. I said he should treat it with care. I doubt that he will.",
   "caveat_stands": "The caveat stands. Kalsa keeps asking what lies behind it. I have told them they will know when I do.",
   "shared_stands": "It is with Kalsa and can't be taken back. Whatever you tell me now is for the record.",
   "counter": "I have told Kalsa that Kordav is running a campaign against Vell, and that our walk-in is part of it. Their minister was silent for some time, and then thanked me. I have told an ally that its best witness was sent. I am relying on you.",
   "counter_stands": "The warning stands. Keep building the file. Kalsa will ask for it after the vote.",
   "restrict": "We stop sharing, and we keep him talking. If he was sent, I want to know what else he was sent to say. I will not say that to Kalsa on medium confidence.",
   "restrict_stands": "Nothing is leaving the building, and he is still talking. I need high confidence before I call Kalsa.",
   "end": "We have ended the debriefing. He has been told there will be no resettlement. He took it more calmly than I expected.",
   "end_stands": "The debriefing is over. He is no longer our case.",
   "withdraw_warning": "I have withdrawn the warning I gave Kalsa. Their minister asked whether this happens often. I said that it does not.",
   "listen": "We keep listening. Nothing leaves this building.",
   "frozen": "Kalsa has asked us to stay out of it, and for now we will. Nothing goes to them. Keep him talking.",
   "rebuff": "On your assessment I told Kalsa, after one interview, that the walk-in was sent. Kalsa believes we are protecting Vell for reasons of our own, and has asked us to stay out of it. For three days we will."},
 "triggers": {
   "withdrew-caveat": "Credibility −1. She had to withdraw the caveated warning she passed on your assessment.",
   "hedging": "Credibility −1. Low again. \"I can't take 'perhaps' to a minister twice.\""},
 "sources": {
   "S-LANTERN": "Says he is a major on Kordav's General Staff planning directorate. No track record. What he volunteers reaches you daily. Anything you want tested, you have to ask.",
   "S-STATION": "Our station in Aubrec. It runs the debriefing and reports what happens in the room.",
   "S-FINCH": "Inside Kordav's defence ministry for six years, and reliable. A meeting takes two days to arrange. The ministry is a dangerous place to be seen asking questions.",
   "S-WREN": "Elsin's military intelligence. It passes what it wants us to see, and answers requests. Some of its officers have long distrusted Vell."},
 "items": {
   "R-1-01": "Walk-in at the embassy, 0710. He asked for asylum. His Kordavan passport matches the officer he says he is: a major on the General Staff's planning directorate.\nHe asked for asylum for himself only. Of his wife, he said she \"will be fine.\"\nHe asked that his presence be kept secret \"for my safety.\"",
   "R-1-02": "First session, volunteered: he served on the IRON HARVEST planning cell. He describes the \"northern option,\" the 0400 switch to contingency accounts and the staging area in Dolan forest.\nAccess claimed: direct, as a member of the cell.",
   "R-1-03": "First session, volunteered: Deputy Defence Minister Vell has been a Kordavan agent for four years.\nAccess claimed: he learned it from \"colleagues in the Bureau,\" Kordav's security service.\nHe will give the details of Vell's meetings with his handler once his resettlement is guaranteed.",
   "R-1-04": "Kalsa: Elsin's parliament will vote on the 12th on a no-confidence motion against Deputy Defence Minister Aron Vell, who negotiated the task force agreement. The opposition calls him \"too close to foreign militaries.\"",
   "R-1-05": "Embassy notice: the annual fire drill is on Thursday. Staff are reminded that the interview room is not a fire exit.",
   "R-2-01": "Second session. His account of his career matches yesterday's almost word for word.\nDebriefer comment: \"unusually calm for a man who has just abandoned his country.\"",
   "R-2-02": "Second session, volunteered: he asks whether we have anyone in Kordav's defence ministry who could vouch for him, and offers the names of officers who \"know him well.\"",
   "R-2-03": "State television, evening programme: a long segment on \"corruption\" in Elsin's defence ministry, naming Deputy Minister Vell.\nNothing about any missing officer.",
   "R-2-04": "Aubrec: the city's metro workers strike tomorrow, as they do most winters. Embassy staff are advised to work from home.",
   "R-3-01": "Elsin's service has heard talk that \"someone from Kordav\" is speaking about \"a traitor in our ministry,\" and asks us for details.\nSourcing: hearsay, not attributed.\nThe liaison officer adds that \"some here have long doubted Vell.\"",
   "R-3-02": "Third session. He produces what he calls his proof: a photocopy of a Bureau payment ledger for an agent codenamed ORIOLE. The payment dates match Vell's trips abroad.",
   "R-3-03": "Document examination of the ledger copy.\nPaper and stamps: genuine Bureau stock.\nDates: every one matches a trip on Vell's public ministry schedule. None matches his private travel.",
   "R-3-04": "Kalsa: parliament has moved the no-confidence vote up from the 12th to the 10th.",
   "R-3-05": "Trending in Kordav: a video of a general's wedding. Forty minutes, three brass bands.",
   "R-4-01": "Fourth session. He insists the allegation reach Elsin before the vote: \"After the vote it will be too late. He will purge the honest officers.\"",
   "R-4-02": "Fourth session. He asks for resettlement \"for my family too.\"\nDebriefer comment: the first time he has raised his family himself since Day 1.",
   "R-4-03": "An Elsin opposition MP posts that \"a Kordavan officer who has defected to an allied service\" has evidence against Vell.\nThe MP names no source.",
   "R-4-04": "State television repeats its segment on Vell, with a new section: \"questions about his foreign contacts.\"",
   "R-4-05": "Security office: the phishing campaign is back, now as \"Updated_Per_Diem_Rates_v2.xlsm\". The rates have still not gone up.",
   "R-5-01": "State television, lead item: a Kordavan officer was \"kidnapped by Western intelligence\" in Aubrec. It gives LANTERN's true name.",
   "R-5-02": "Fifth session. He asks to record a statement for the press, to be released \"tomorrow morning.\"",
   "R-5-03": "Elsin's service passes: the opposition's motion now cites \"allied intelligence.\"\nIt asks whether we have shared anything with anyone.",
   "R-5-04": "Aubrec: the metro strike is over.",
   "C-12": "Session on his access.\nAsked what his directorate does day to day, he describes exercise planning, movement tables and fuel allocations, in detail and accurately.\nAsked how a planning officer comes to know what is in Bureau agent files: \"People talk. You hear things at dinners.\"",
   "C-10": "Session on the Vell details. He says Vell met his Bureau handler at the Hotel Sorel in Kalsa, room 412, on 14 March.\nAsked how he knows the room number, he can't say: \"It was known.\"",
   "C-13": "Session on his family.\nAsked about his wife, he repeats that she \"will be fine\" because she \"knows nothing about my work.\"\nAsked whether the Bureau will question her: it \"doesn't bother the families of planning officers.\"",
   "C-09": "Session on the ledger.\nAsked how he got it out: he photographed it in the Bureau's central registry.\nAsked how a General Staff planner got into the registry: a friend signed him in. He won't name the friend.",
   "C-04": "At a meeting, source reports: LANTERN is known in the ministry as a mid-level planning officer, \"nowhere near counterintelligence files.\"\nSource asked why we wanted to know. He mentioned that someone else asked him about LANTERN's friends last week.\nAccess: direct. He knows the directorate.",
   "C-08": "The defence ministry's published staff directory lists LANTERN's true name under the General Staff planning directorate, logistics branch.\nThe Bureau is a separate service with its own directory. His name is not in it.",
   "C-06": "Bureau headquarters to its office in Aubrec. Administrative net, routine precedence.\n\"Suspend contact with all persons known to [LANTERN's true name]. Officers KARSA, DOVIN and TREL to return to the capital for consultations.\"",
   "C-11": "Bureau headquarters administrative net: routine traffic only. Nothing further about LANTERN. The recall of KARSA, DOVIN and TREL is not mentioned again.",
   "C-01": "Bureau office in Aubrec, its own traffic: routine.\nEvery officer on the duty roster is still at post. Nobody has been called home.\nNo change to codes or procedures since LANTERN walked in.\nOne circular from headquarters reminds staff of \"media discipline this week.\"",
   "C-07": "Kordav's embassy in Aubrec: routine traffic.\nIts security officer has been told to \"keep distance from the matter.\"\nNot seen: any effort to find a missing officer.",
   "C-02": "His wife's accounts are posting normally: a school concert on the 6th, plans for a weekend at the lake.\nNot seen: any sign she has been questioned or stopped from travelling.",
   "C-03": "Three weeks ago The Kalsa Courier, a paper close to the opposition, ran an unsourced story: Vell met \"a foreign businessman\" at the Hotel Sorel, room 412, on 14 March.",
   "C-05": "In answer to our request: Elsin confirms Vell was at the Hotel Sorel on 14 March, at a meeting with a defence contractor logged by his own office.\nThe liaison officer adds that \"some here have long doubted him.\""},
 "notes": {
   ("vasko", "R-1-04"): "The vote is the only story in Kalsa this week. Whatever arrives before it will be used against Vell, whoever sent it.",
   ("vasko", "R-2-01"): "Calm proves nothing. Staff officers rehearse everything. And what he gave us on IRON HARVEST was right to the hour. I think he's real.",
   ("vasko", "R-3-02"): "That is real Bureau paper. A man inventing a story for a visa doesn't get hold of it.",
   ("vasko", "R-4-02"): "He's asking about his family now because it has sunk in that he isn't going home. That's normal by the fourth day.",
   ("vasko", "R-5-01"): "So Kordav has noticed he's gone. This is how a real defection looks from the other side: silence, then outrage.",
   ("halden", "R-1-02"): "I checked what he gave us on IRON HARVEST against our own file. We already held all of it. It's accurate, and none of it is new.",
   ("halden", "R-3-03"): "The dates are all public trips. If I were paying an agent, I'd do it on the trips nobody could look up.",
   ("halden", "R-4-03"): "We told nobody. Elsin's service had talk of \"someone from Kordav,\" nothing about a defector. So where did the MP get the word \"defected\"?",
   ("halden", "C-06"): "A recall after a compromise goes out on the operational channel at the highest precedence. This went on the admin net, routine, with the officers named. I think it was meant to be read.",
   ("halden", "C-10"): "An exact room number, and he can't say how he knows it. That usually comes from a briefing or a newspaper."},
 "analysts": {
   "vasko": "He settles on a reading early. He began well, with the vote. Then LANTERN's IRON HARVEST details persuaded him the man was genuine, and nothing afterwards moved him.",
   "halden": "Careful, and slow. Her notes come a day after the item. She wasn't wrong once, and each time you had already briefed without her."},
 "endings": {
   "burned": "Elsin arrests Vell on the morning of the vote. Six months later the ledger is shown to be a forgery built from his public schedule. Kalsa stops taking allied intelligence at its word. Kordav got everything it sent LANTERN for.",
   "sidelined": "The caveated warning leaks within a day and is quoted in the opposition's speech. Vell resigns \"to clear his name.\" Elsin's reformers lose their strongest voice.",
   "exposed": "Elsin's defence minister stands up in parliament with your warning: Kordav is running a smear, and its \"defector\" was sent. The motion fails by forty votes. LANTERN's statement is never released. Two Bureau officers are recalled from Aubrec, and this time it is real.",
   "survived": "Your warning reaches Kalsa the night before the vote. The motion fails by three. Vell keeps his job, and the allegation follows him.",
   "shelved": "Nothing left the building. The opposition runs with the MP's claim anyway, and Vell survives by five votes. He never learns how close it was, or who was behind it."},
 "script": [
   "LANTERN arrived with a gift and a condition. The gift was IRON HARVEST: all of it true, and worth nothing to Kordav, because the operation had been known for three months. The condition was resettlement before details. He said the Vell allegation came from \"colleagues in the Bureau.\" A planning officer doesn't see the Bureau's agent files.",
   "If you pressed him for details, he gave you room 412. The Kalsa Courier had printed it three weeks before he walked in. His question about who could vouch for him was his second task: to find out who we have in the ministry. And if you were reading the Bureau's headquarters net, you saw a recall that never happened. Kordav put it where it believed we were listening.",
   "The ledger was genuine paper carrying a forged story. Every payment fell on a trip anyone could look up, and none on a trip Vell hadn't announced.",
   "The deadline he pressed was the vote, and only Kordav needed the allegation to land before it. The MP's word \"defected\" could only have come from LANTERN's side.",
   "Kordav said nothing about a missing officer for four days, then announced a kidnapping on the eve of the vote. The same day, the kidnapped man asked to record a press statement. Through all of it the Bureau's office in Aubrec changed nothing."],
 "lessons": [
   "True details show access. They say nothing about intent. LANTERN's account of IRON HARVEST was accurate and cost Kordav nothing.",
   "Check a claim against access. A General Staff planner had no way to see Bureau agent files.",
   "Count where a detail comes from. Room 412 had been in a newspaper for three weeks.",
   "Watch how the other side reacts, on every channel you can reach. A service that has really lost an officer changes its codes and questions his family. The Bureau did neither.",
   "The adversary knows some of what you collect. The recall was sent on the net Kordav believed we read.",
   "Asking can be seen. The Bureau was watching the ministry for anyone who asked about LANTERN."],
 "burn": ["FINCH didn't come to the meeting. His signal site has not been serviced."],
}
